{:edb/db-before-t 819 :edb/db-after-t 820 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534132 50 #inst "2026-10-11T16:57:30.730Z" 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186162011 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411461 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186162012 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411460 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186162012 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411462 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186162668 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161300 13194139534132 17592186045417 false] [17592186162668 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411458 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186162668 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186162668 1071 "Find the principal axes of the curve $x_{1}^{2} + 4x_{1}x_{2} + x_{2}^{2} = 48$." 13194139534132 17592186045417 false] [17592186162668 1071 "Find the principal axes of the curve ${x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=48.$" 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186162668 1074 "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=48\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186162719 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161606 13194139534132 17592186045417 false] [17592186162719 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411459 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186162719 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186162719 1071 "Consider the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and the quadratic curve $x^{T}Ax = 2$. Given that $A$ has eigenvalues $λ_{1} = 5$ and $λ_{2} = 0$, find the principal axes of the curve." 13194139534132 17592186045417 false] [17592186162719 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=2.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ find the principal axes of the curve." 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186162719 1074 "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=2\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking 