6411444 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411459 1079 #uuid "de22cbdd-1887-52e7-a256-745226952983" 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411460 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411455 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411460 1069 "q-95002" 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411460 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411460 1071 "Which curve is defined by the equation $3{x}_{1}^{2}+10{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}=32?$" 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411460 1072 #uuid "6abf962b-d107-56b7-9263-80678639c14a" 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411460 1074 "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+10{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.  \n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 5 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda -16 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =8,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $8{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}.$  \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2} & =32 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes  $2$ and $4.$" 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411461 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411457 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411461 1069 "q-95042" 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411461 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411461 1071 "Find the principal axes of the curve ${x}_{1}^{2}+12{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}=40.$" 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411461 1072 #uuid "d9ebdd12-b59c-52db-97fa-216d502d55a4" 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411461 1074 "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+12{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=40\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-8.$ \n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)1I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-(-8)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411456 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411462 1069 "q-94985" 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411462 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411462 1071 "Which curve is defined by the equation $9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}=10?$" 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411462 1072 #uuid "f8d1da1f-2237-5249-8b88-e74004bb86c1" 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411462 1074 "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.  \n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-15\\lambda +50 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $5{y}_{1}^{2}+10{y}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}+10{y}_{2}^{2} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{1} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{1} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $1.$" 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411463 1058 #edb/ref 17592186411467 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411463 1062 #uuid "19ff91ae-0eac-5f3c-95d1-bb03c7aa3351" 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411463 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162668 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411464 1058 #edb/ref 17592186411466 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411464 1062 #uuid "226f8cb7-5c22-5a4b-8d5c-99f40fa38b6f" 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411464 1063 #edb/ref 17592186411460 13194139534132 17592186045417 true] [17592186411465 1058 #edb/ref 17592186411464 13194139534132 17592186